I was surprised to find recently that \max, \min, \lim, \limsup, etc
are set in plain TeX and LaTeX without limits in text maths mode.
Not having been on an analysis course for some years, I was unaware of
this fact until now.

Are there ANY mathematicians who would place {n\to\infty} as a
SUBSCRIPT on \lim? I would be very surprised. Similarly for \max.

I would suggest that the \limits should be given explicitly for these
operators at least.

\def\lim{\mathop{\operator@font lim}\limits}
\def\limsup{\mathop{\operator@font lim\,sup}\limits}
\def\liminf{\mathop{\operator@font lim\,inf}\limits}
\def\max{\mathop{\operator@font max}\limits}
\def\min{\mathop{\operator@font min}\limits}
\def\sup{\mathop{\operator@font sup}\limits}
\def\inf{\mathop{\operator@font inf}\limits}

(Substitute \rm in plain TeX.)

On another point, in NFSS the private command \operator@font is used
in operators. Could I suggest that a PUBLIC command be used, so that
the casual user can easily define his own operators?
(I am assuming that LaTeX3 will not appear for some considerable time,
eg, 2 years.)

Mike Piff
